Therefore in this paper we focus on schedulers that can utilize
the parent-child TB data reuse. Higher shared footprint ratio
reveals better potential locality between the direct parent and
the child TBs which may exist both spatially and temporally
as described in the following:
Temporal Locality: A common practice in using the dynamic parallelism model for irregular applications is that the
parent TB performs the necessary computation to generate the
data, passes the data pointers (usually stored in the global
memory) to the child and invokes the child to continue the
computation. The reuse of the parent-generated data by the
child TBs results in good temporal locality as long as the
execution of the child TBs is “soon enough” after the parent.
The parent-child shared footprint ratio shown in Figure 2
demonstrate the potential existence of such temporal locality.
